# Line endings.
#
# This repo is edited from Windows and from a Linux sandbox. Without this file git saw
# 22 result files as modified with 4,808 insertions and 4,808 deletions — an exactly equal
# count, which is the signature of a CRLF/LF change rather than a content change.
# `git diff --ignore-cr-at-eol` confirmed it: nothing in the numbers moved.
#
# That matters more here than in most projects. This paper's central claim is that every
# reported figure is read from a result file, and a diff that shows every line of every
# result file as changed makes a real change indistinguishable from noise. A reviewer
# auditing the history could not tell the two apart, and neither could we.
#
# Normalise text to LF in the repository; git checks out the platform's convention.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary — never touch these.
*.png binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary

# Result files: keep them diffable and normalised, so a genuine change to a number is
# visible as a one-line diff.
*.csv  text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.jsonl text eol=lf
*.md   text eol=lf
*.tex  text eol=lf
*.bib  text eol=lf
*.py   text eol=lf

# After adding this file, run once to renormalise the index:
#   git add --renormalize .
#   git commit -m "Normalise line endings"
